    {
      "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-47d",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "47D Appeals to High Court may not be brought",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2021a00013rec01#sch-1-sec-47d"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "c2021a00013rec01.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "c2021a00013rec01.pdf, p.31",
      "values:originalText": "(1) An appeal must not be brought directly to the High Court from a\ndecree of the following courts exercising jurisdiction under this\nAct:\n(a) a court of summary jurisdiction of a State or Territory;\n(b) a Family Court of a State;\n(c) a Supreme Court of a State or Territory constituted by a\nsingle Judge.\nNote:\nIn relation to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, see\nsections 55 and 155 of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of\nAustralia Act 2021.\n(2) If, apart from this subsection, subsection (1) is to any extent\ninconsistent with section 73 of the Constitution, this Act has effect\nas if the words “, except by special leave of the High Court” were\ninserted after the words “this Act” in subsection (1).\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments relating to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia",
      "skos:definition": "(1) An appeal must not be brought directly to the High Court from a\ndecree of the following courts exercising jurisdiction under this\nAct:\n(a) a court of summary jurisdiction of a State or Territory;\n(b) a Family Court of a State;\n(c) a Supreme Court of a State or Territory constituted by a\nsingle Judge.\nNote:\nIn relation to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, see\nsections 55 and 155 of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of\nAustralia Act 2021.\n(2) If, apart from this subsection, subsection (1) is to any extent\ninconsistent with section 73 of the Constitution, this Act has effect\nas if the words “, except by special leave of the High Court” were\ninserted after the words “this Act” in subsection (1).\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments relating to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-47d-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-47d-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "0e1382591cfadf05a1f548647e37a5852dc534296fb5afa8852b6a7af15c353a"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2021a00013rec01#sch-1-sec-47d",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "47D Appeals to High Court may not be brought",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 31,
      "values:originalText": "(1) An appeal must not be brought directly to the High Court from a\ndecree of the following courts exercising jurisdiction under this\nAct:\n(a) a court of summary jurisdiction of a State or Territory;\n(b) a Family Court of a State;\n(c) a Supreme Court of a State or Territory constituted by a\nsingle Judge.\nNote:\nIn relation to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, see\nsections 55 and 155 of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of\nAustralia Act 2021.\n(2) If, apart from this subsection, subsection (1) is to any extent\ninconsistent with section 73 of the Constitution, this Act has effect\nas if the words “, except by special leave of the High Court” were\ninserted after the words “this Act” in subsection (1).\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments relating to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia"
    },
