    {
      "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-13a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "13A Authorised Judges may manage classes of proceedings",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2021a00013rec01#sch-1-sec-13a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "c2021a00013rec01.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "c2021a00013rec01.pdf, p.49",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Chief Justice may, by written instrument, authorise a Judge to\nmanage such class or classes of proceedings as may be specified:\n(a) in the instrument; or\n(b) by the Rules of Court.\n(2) In managing a class or classes of proceedings, a Judge is subject to\nany direction from the Chief Justice.\n(3) A Judge may be authorised even though the Judge is not assigned\nto a Division.\n(4) The authorisation of a Judge does not affect the rank, title, status\nand precedence as a Judge that the Judge had immediately before\nany such authorisation.\n(5) If a direction under subsection (2) is given in writing, the direction\nis not a legislative instrument.\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments relating to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Chief Justice may, by written instrument, authorise a Judge to\nmanage such class or classes of proceedings as may be specified:\n(a) in the instrument; or\n(b) by the Rules of Court.\n(2) In managing a class or classes of proceedings, a Judge is subject to\nany direction from the Chief Justice.\n(3) A Judge may be authorised even though the Judge is not assigned\nto a Division.\n(4) The authorisation of a Judge does not affect the rank, title, status\nand precedence as a Judge that the Judge had immediately before\nany such authorisation.\n(5) If a direction under subsection (2) is given in writing, the direction\nis not a legislative instrument.\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments relating to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-13a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-13a-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-13a-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-13a-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-13a-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "89254e869c1df0d8bf41871fedff6442dea3ec7991fa11af23190a8e391ac276"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2021a00013rec01#sch-1-sec-13a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "13A Authorised Judges may manage classes of proceedings",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 49,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Chief Justice may, by written instrument, authorise a Judge to\nmanage such class or classes of proceedings as may be specified:\n(a) in the instrument; or\n(b) by the Rules of Court.\n(2) In managing a class or classes of proceedings, a Judge is subject to\nany direction from the Chief Justice.\n(3) A Judge may be authorised even though the Judge is not assigned\nto a Division.\n(4) The authorisation of a Judge does not affect the rank, title, status\nand precedence as a Judge that the Judge had immediately before\nany such authorisation.\n(5) If a direction under subsection (2) is given in writing, the direction\nis not a legislative instrument.\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments relating to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia"
    },
