      "cml:conclusionText": "Consultation must occur before making Rules of Court"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "32AC(6) Discretionary transfer of civil proceedings from the Federal",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2021a00013rec01#sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "c2021a00013rec01.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "c2021a00013rec01.pdf, p.60",
      "values:originalText": "(6) In deciding whether to transfer a proceeding from the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), the Court must\nhave regard to:\n(a) any Rules of Court made for the purposes of subsection (4);\nand\n(b) whether proceedings in respect of an associated matter are\npending in the Court; and\n(c) whether the resources of the Court are sufficient to hear and\ndetermine the proceeding; and\n(d) the interests of the administration of justice.",
      "skos:definition": "(6) In deciding whether to transfer a proceeding from the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), the Court must\nhave regard to:\n(a) any Rules of Court made for the purposes of subsection (4);\nand\n(b) whether proceedings in respect of an associated matter are\npending in the Court; and\n(c) whether the resources of the Court are sufficient to hear and\ndetermine the proceeding; and\n(d) the interests of the administration of justice.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6-logic-alleninterval-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6-logic-ltl-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6-logic-deontic-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2021a00013rec01-sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6-logic-deontic-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "eb9f142db8b6aa6d3f285164bb4da8b41c3c8c14d61f147a5158d780655ae5c6",
      "skos:note": "Court must consider certain factors when deciding to transfer a proceeding."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2021a00013rec01#sch-1-sec-32ac-ss-6",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "32AC(6) Discretionary transfer of civil proceedings from the Federal",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 60,
      "values:originalText": "(6) In deciding whether to transfer a proceeding from the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), the Court must\nhave regard to:\n(a) any Rules of Court made for the purposes of subsection (4);\nand\n(b) whether proceedings in respect of an associated matter are\npending in the Court; and\n(c) whether the resources of the Court are sufficient to hear and\ndetermine the proceeding; and\n(d) the interests of the administration of justice."
    },
    {
